FAAD2 compatibility library
==========================

This package provides the legacy NeAACDec API and libfaad.so.2 ABI as a thin
shim over libfaad3. It builds only the library; the CLI ships with libfaad3.
Output channel order and positions follow FAAD2.

Build and install libfaad3 first (https://github.com/nschimme/faac, branch
libfaad, Meson option -Ddecoder=true). Then, with CMake and pkg-config installed:

    PKG_CONFIG_PATH=/path/to/libfaad3/lib/pkgconfig cmake -S . -B build
    cmake --build build
    cmake --install build

Use -DBUILD_SHARED_LIBS=OFF for a static library. Static consumers must also
link libfaad3; pkg-config --libs --static faad2 supplies the dependencies.

Main/LTP/SSR/LD/ER profiles, DRM, fixed-point decoding, LATM, ADIF, and raw AAC
without AudioSpecificConfig (ASC) are no longer supported. useOldADTSFormat
and dontUpSampleImplicitSBR are ignored. LC-only streams at or below 24 kHz
are reported at their real sample rate and are not upsampled to twice the
rate. The legacy InitDRM entry point remains exported but reports failure.

The output format and downMatrix setting are fixed when the decoder is
initialized (NeAACDecInit/NeAACDecInit2); a change made later applies at the
next NeAACDecPostSeekReset or NeAACDecInit.

Dynamic range control is applied exactly as FAAD2 did (Debian builds it in by
default): full compression, and streams that carry a programme reference level
are normalised to -20 dBFS. A stream that carries dynamic_range_info but no
programme reference level is played 20 dB quieter, as before; callers may have
worked around that.

Compatibility and migration
---------------------------

Programs that call this API keep their FAAD2 behaviour, including its quirks:
the first decoded frame reports no samples, channels come in bitstream order
with FAAD2 positions, the 5/6-channel downmix and the error numbers are
unchanged, a decode call searches up to 768 bytes for an ADTS syncword, and the
output format and downMatrix settings are read when the decoder initialises.
Known differences from FAAD2:
- LC-only streams at or below 24 kHz are reported and emitted at their real
  rate; FAAD2 doubled the rate and upsampled them.
- For implicit SBR in a raw/MP4 configuration at or below 24 kHz, NeAACDecInit2
  reports the core rate; the frames carry the doubled rate (ADTS is reported
  correctly). Use explicit signalling, or read the rate from the frames.
- Init reports the channel configuration index FAAD2 did for 7.1 (two for
  ADTS, seven for a raw/MP4 configuration); every decoded frame reports the
  real eight.
- A corrupt or false-sync ADTS frame yields an error number from the same table
  but not always the same one as FAAD2.
- 32-bit output clamps at positive full scale instead of wrapping.
- Samples differ at the level of decoder arithmetic (about 50 dB agreement
  between any two AAC decoders on noise-substituted content). HE-AAC output
  agrees with FAAD2 only to about 20 dB, as it does between any two decoders:
  the high band is regenerated, so it matches in level and shape, not sample
  for sample.
- Main/LTP/SSR/LD/ER profiles, DRM, fixed-point, LATM, ADIF and raw AAC without
  an AudioSpecificConfig are not supported; useOldADTSFormat and
  dontUpSampleImplicitSBR are ignored.

New code should use libfaad3 (faad3.h) directly. It reports the PCM format it
really emits, returns concealed audio with flags instead of errors, has a
stable struct-size-checked ABI, and drops these quirks. The porting guide in
libfaad3's docs/libfaad.md lists every legacy behaviour and its replacement.
